---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const sections = [
  { id: 'tldr', label: 'TL;DR' },
  { id: 'context', label: 'Context' },
  { id: 'observation', label: 'The Observation' },
  { id: 'mental-model', label: 'Mental Models' },
  { id: 'workflow', label: 'The Real Workflow' },
  { id: 'strategy', label: 'Strategy Shift' },
  { id: 'questions', label: 'The Right Questions' },
  { id: 'reflection', label: 'Reflection' },
];
---

<Base title="Work Management — When a Document Becomes a Workflow" description="A product case study on shifting from document management to work management by understanding the workflow that surrounds every document.">
  <article class="wrap section case-study">
    <a href="/projects" class="meta back">← Back to Projects</a>

    <header class="cs-hero">
      <span class="cs-badge meta">📄 Case Study</span>
      <h1>Work Management — When a Document Becomes a Workflow</h1>
      <p class="cs-lede">The goal isn't to help customers manage more things. It's to help them finish the thing they're actually trying to get done.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="cs-grid">
      <aside class="cs-sidebar">
        <div class="cs-meta-card">
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label meta">Year</span>
            <span class="cs-meta-value">2024 — Present</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label meta">Role</span>
            <span class="cs-meta-value">Lead Product Manager, Progress ShareFile</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label meta">Domain</span>
            <span class="cs-meta-value">Work Management · Document Workflow · Enterprise</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label meta">Skills</span>
            <ul class="cs-skill-list">
              <li>Product Strategy</li>
              <li>Enterprise Product</li>
              <li>Workflow Design</li>
              <li>Systems Thinking</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <nav class="cs-toc" aria-label="Case study sections">
          <p class="cs-toc-title meta">On this page</p>
          <ul class="cs-toc-list">
            {sections.map((s) => (
              <li>
                <a href={`#${s.id}`} class="cs-toc-link" data-section={s.id}>{s.label}</a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </nav>
      </aside>

      <div class="cs-content">

        <section class="cs-section" id="tldr">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">01</span>
            <h2>TL;DR</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body">
            <div class="cs-tldr-box">
              <p>Enterprise customers don't experience products the way we organize them internally. A document isn't just a file — it's part of a request, an approval, a compliance requirement, a deliverable. The real work isn't managing the document; it's <strong>completing the workflow around the document</strong>. The shift from document management to work management means designing for outcomes, not objects — making disconnected capabilities feel like one continuous path toward getting the job done.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="cs-section" id="context">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">02</span>
            <h2>Context</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>One of the things I find most interesting about enterprise software is that customers rarely experience the product the way we design it internally.</p>
            <p>Inside a product organization, we might have a document-management team, a tasks team, a collaboration team, an eSignature team, and a workflow team. Each capability has its own roadmap, metrics, engineering team, and product manager.</p>
            <p>But the customer doesn't experience those boundaries. They simply have work they need to complete.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="cs-section" id="observation">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">03</span>
            <h2>The Observation</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>A document is a good example. At first glance, a document is just a file. You upload it, store it, share it, and eventually download it.</p>
            <p>But in a real business process, documents rarely exist in isolation. A document might represent a request, an approval, a compliance requirement, a customer deliverable, a contract, or a decision.</p>
            <p>Someone needs to request the document. Someone else needs to provide it. Another person needs to review it. Someone might need to make corrections. Another person may need to sign it. Eventually, somebody needs to know whether the entire process is complete.</p>
            <blockquote class="cs-quote">At that point, the document isn't really the work. The workflow around the document is the work.</blockquote>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="cs-section" id="mental-model">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">04</span>
            <h2>Mental Models</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>The traditional mental model for document software is largely <strong>object-centric</strong>: here's your file, here's your folder, here's your permission, here's your share link.</p>
            <p>But the customer's mental model is often <strong>outcome-centric</strong>.</p>
            <p>They aren't thinking, "I need to upload a document." They're thinking, "I need to collect all the documents required to complete this process."</p>
            <p>Those sound like similar problems until you map the actual workflow.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="cs-section" id="workflow">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">05</span>
            <h2>The Real Workflow</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>Imagine a business collecting documents from a customer. They might create a folder, send an email requesting the files, explain what is required, wait for a response, follow up when something is missing, review what comes back, request corrections, obtain signatures, and finally mark the process complete.</p>
            <p>From the product's perspective, those could be several separate features.</p>
            <p>From the customer's perspective, it's one job: <strong>collect and complete the required documents.</strong></p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="cs-section" id="strategy">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">06</span>
            <h2>Strategy Shift</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>That difference changes the product strategy. Instead of asking how we can make each individual capability better, we can ask how we can make the journey between those capabilities more seamless.</p>
            <p>A request can create a task. A document can become part of that task. A review can trigger another action. A signature can represent a completion milestone. An activity stream can provide the context around what happened.</p>
            <p>The individual features start becoming building blocks inside a larger workflow.</p>
            <p>The opportunity isn't necessarily to build another generic project-management product. It's to understand where work naturally happens inside the business and reduce the friction between the systems and actions required to complete it.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="cs-section" id="questions">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">07</span>
            <h2>The Right Questions</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p>That requires thinking about the customer's job rather than the product's information architecture:</p>
            <p>Where does the work begin? Who owns the next step? What information needs to move between people? Where do handoffs fail? What causes delays? What does "done" actually mean?</p>
            <p>Those questions often reveal product opportunities that aren't visible when you look at features individually.</p>
            <p>A file is an object. A document is information. But once people start acting around that document, it becomes part of a workflow.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="cs-section cs-section--highlight" id="reflection">
          <div class="cs-section-label">
            <span class="cs-section-num meta">✦</span>
            <h2>Reflection</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-section-body prose">
            <p><strong>The product opportunity is to make the workflow feel less like a collection of disconnected tools and more like one continuous path toward an outcome.</strong></p>
            <p>The goal isn't to help customers manage more things.</p>
            <p>It's to help them finish the thing they're actually trying to get done.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style>
    .back { display: inline-block; text-decoration: none; margin-bottom: 2rem; }

    .cs-hero { padding-bottom: 2.5rem; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink); margin-bottom: 3rem; }
    .cs-badge { display: inline-block; padding: 0.3rem 0.7rem; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 12%, transparent); color: var(--accent); border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 30%, transparent); margin-bottom: 1.2rem; font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.04em; }
    .cs-hero h1 { max-width: 22ch; }
    .cs-lede { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 1.25rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 52ch; line-height: 1.5; }

    .cs-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 15rem 1fr; gap: 4rem; }

    .cs-sidebar { position: sticky; top: 5rem; align-self: start; max-height: calc(100vh - 6rem); overflow-y: auto; }
    .cs-meta-card { border-top: 2px solid var(--accent); padding-top: 1.2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.2rem; }
    .cs-meta-label { display: block; color: var(--ink-soft); margin-bottom: 0.3rem; font-size: 0.68rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; }
    .cs-meta-value { font-size: 0.92rem; }
    .cs-skill-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.25rem; font-size: 0.88rem; }

    .cs-toc { margin-top: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding-top: 1rem; }
    .cs-toc-title { font-size: 0.68rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--ink-soft); margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
    .cs-toc-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.15rem; }
    .cs-toc-link { display: block; padding: 0.35rem 0.7rem; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--ink-soft); text-decoration: none; border-left: 2px solid transparent; transition: color 0.15s ease, border-color 0.15s ease; }
    .cs-toc-link:hover { color: var(--ink); }
    .cs-toc-link.is-active { color: var(--ink); border-left-color: var(--accent); font-weight: 500; }

    .cs-section { padding-bottom: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); scroll-margin-top: 5rem; }
    .cs-section:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
    .cs-section-label { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.8rem; margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
    .cs-section-num { color: var(--accent); font-size: 0.8rem; flex-shrink: 0; }
    .cs-section-label h2 { font-size: 1.3rem; margin: 0; }
    .cs-section-body p { margin-top: 1rem; max-width: 58ch; line-height: 1.75; }
    .cs-section-body p:first-child { margin-top: 0; }

    .cs-tldr-box { padding: 1.5rem; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 4%, transparent); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); }
    .cs-tldr-box p { font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0; max-width: 58ch; }

    .cs-quote { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.4; color: var(--ink); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding-left: 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 1.5rem 0; max-width: 36ch; }

    .cs-section--highlight { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 5%, transparent); padding: 2rem; border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 20%, transparent); border-bottom: none; }

    @media (max-width: 768px) {
      .cs-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2rem; }
      .cs-sidebar { position: static; max-height: none; }
      .cs-toc { display: none; }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    function initScrollspy() {
      const links = document.querySelectorAll('.cs-toc-link');
      const sectionIds = Array.from(links).map(l => l.getAttribute('data-section'));
      const sections = sectionIds.map(id => document.getElementById(id)).filter(Boolean);
      if (!sections.length) return;

      const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
        entries.forEach(entry => {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            links.forEach(l => l.classList.remove('is-active'));
            const active = document.querySelector(`.cs-toc-link[data-section="${entry.target.id}"]`);
            if (active) active.classList.add('is-active');
          }
        });
      }, { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -70% 0px' });

      sections.forEach(s => observer.observe(s));
    }
    initScrollspy();
    document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', initScrollspy);
  </script>
</Base>
